          %p
            Abstract bicategories of partial maps, parallel to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory+of+relations"} bicategories of relations
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Carb87"} Carboni 87
          %h5#in_terms_of_the_maybe_monad In terms of the maybe monad
          %p
            Notice that a partial function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            as
            %a{:href => "#DefinitionInSets"} above
            is (in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"}> coproduct
            ) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            with the point (the singleton)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                  %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi \;\colon\; A \longrightarrow B \coprod \ast
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \hookrightarrow A
            in the
            %a{:href => "#DefinitionInSets"} above
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^*(B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in this preimage,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = \phi(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in A
            is sent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is undefined at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            This in turn is equivalently a morphism in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maybe+monad"} maybe monad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            and with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
            (partial functions with complemented domain).
          %h2#examples_2 Examples
          %p
            In secondary-school mathematics, one often makes functions partial by fiat, just to see if students can calculate the domains of composite functions and the like. This is not (only) busywork, as in applications one often has a function given by a formula that is really valid only on a certain domain. However, in more sophisticated analyses (such as those that Lawvere and his followers propose for physics and synthetic geometry), these domains and the total functions on them become the primary objects of study, with the partial functions being secondary (as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ι
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota
            is seen as merely a way to place
            %em coordinates
            on
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            In analysis, one often considers partial functions whose domains are required to be intervals in the real line, regions of the complex plane, or dense subsets of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            is a partial function whose domain is the set of non-zero elements of the field.
          %h2#the_category_of_sets_and_partial_functions The category of sets and partial functions
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi part
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_part
            of sets and partial functions between them is important for understanding computation. However, one often replaces this with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            category of sets and total functions.
          %p
            Specifically, replace each set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\bot
            of all
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with at most one element, otherwise known as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+map+classifier"} partial map classifier
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            with the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x\}
            and write the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty
            subset as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \to T
            becomes a total function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊥
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\bot \to T_\bot
            such that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            are assigned only to inhabited subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi part
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_part
            is equivalent to the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\bot
            of such sets and functions.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mo ⊥
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⨿
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\bot \cong S \amalg \{\bot\}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructively
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⨿
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\mapsto S \amalg \{\bot\}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maybe+monad"} maybe monad
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\bot
            is its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            %span.newWikiWord
              free
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/free+algebra"} ?
            this category is also equivalent to its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+category"} Eilenberg-Moore category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_*
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+set"} pointed set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\bot
            but (unless one is a constructivist) thinks of this as simply different notation for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Set
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\mapsto S_\bot
            is a monad (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+map+classifier"} partial map classifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\bot
            is its Kleisli category, but it is (probably) no longer true that every algebra is free.
          %p
            For a more sophisticated analysis of computation,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\bot
            can be replaced with a suitable category of domains, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direction"} directed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partially ordered
            sets (DCPOs). The requirement that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            be preserved can then be removed to model lazy computation, but now we are hardly talking about partial functions anymore.
          %h2#algebras_of_partial_functions Algebras of partial functions
          %p
            The functions of high-school mathematics, consisting of real (or complex)-valued functions of one (or two or three) real (or complex) variables, are by default partial functions. As they take values in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} dom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} dom
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∩
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} dom
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dom(f + g) = \dom f \cap \dom g
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiring"} semiring
            in which addition has an identity element (the always-defined constant
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+function"} zero function
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+function"} empty function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig"} rig
            because these two elements are not the same. It has many other interesting properties, such as simultaneous additive and multiplicative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+element"} idempotents
            (the zero functions with arbitrary domains).
          %p
            An axiomatic treatment of such semirings may be found at the end of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Richman12"} Richman 2012
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+partial+endofunctions"} category of partial endofunctions
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+function"} total function
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/restriction+category"} restriction category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+partial+endofunctions"} category of partial endofunctions
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function+type"} partial function type
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multivalued+partial+function"} multivalued partial function
          %h2#References References
          %ul
            %li#Carb87
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aurelio+Carboni"}> Aurelio Carboni
                , Bicategories of partial maps, Cahiers de Topologie et Géométrie Différentielle Catégoriques, 28 no. 2 (1987), p. 111-126 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_1987__28_2_111_0"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Cockett"}> Robin Cockett
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"} Steve Lack
